---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import ManualPager from '../../components/ManualPager.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/github';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Running an analysis"
  description="Load frames, parameters, ROI, and compute in Semper."
  path="/manual/analysis"
>
  <section class="section" style="padding-top: 3rem">
    <p class="crumb">
      <a href={withBase('/manual')}>← Manual</a>
    </p>
    <h2>Running an analysis</h2>
    <p class="sub">From loading frames through ROI, parameters, and Compute.</p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="plain">
        Three steps: load your frames, choose an area and settings, then Compute. Everything else is
        detail on those three.
      </p>
      <Figure
        src="/manual/wizard.svg"
        alt="Wizard steps: load frames, settings, optional sweep, then compute"
        caption="Single mode goes straight to Compute. Sweep adds a setup step for ranges."
        wide
        width={760}
        height={240}
      />

      <h3>Step 1 — Load frames</h3>
      <p>
        <span class="lead-label">In the app:</span> tap each dropzone and pick images.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Photos</strong> is the system picker. <strong>Files</strong> is the only path to RAW
        and DNG (and to picking a video — see
        <a href={withBase('/manual/getting-started')}>Getting started</a>).
      </p>
      <Figure
        src="/manual/screenshots/step1-frames.webp"
        alt="Step 1 with reference frame and three numbered deformed frames"
        caption="Number badges on deformed frames are the analysis order. Use Sort before you continue."
        phone
        width={460}
        height={1022}
      />
      <p>
        <span class="lead-label">Why it matters:</span> the number badge on each deformed frame is its
        order, and that order is used everywhere afterward — frame 1 here is frame 1 in every result
        and export.
      </p>

      <h4 class="minor">Getting the order right</h4>
      <p class="detail">
        Tap the sort icon to reorder. There is no way back to the picker&apos;s original order once you
        sort, so set it before you Compute. (With a single deformed frame the control is hidden.)
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Sort</th>
            <th>Use when</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Name · A–Z / Z–A</td>
            <td>Filenames carry the sequence</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Date · oldest / newest</td>
            <td>Filenames don&apos;t — uses capture time, then EXIF</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Manual</td>
            <td>Neither works — drag the thumbnails into order</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>Step 2 — Settings</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Region of interest</strong> — defaults to the full image. Edit opens the ROI
          editor.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Single</strong> or <strong>Sweep</strong> — Single solves every frame once. Sweep
          solves one frame across many parameter combinations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Advanced parameters</strong> — subset, step, strain window, kernel. If you copied a
          combination from a sweep lattice, a <strong>Paste params</strong> chip appears beside
          <strong>Reset</strong> and fills all three in one tap (the screen scrolls them into view).
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>Then tap <strong>Compute</strong>.</p>

      <h3>While it runs</h3>
      <p>Points solved and convergence update live.</p>
      <p>
        <strong>Cancel</strong> stops at once — nothing is kept. Keep the app open; there is no
        resume if Android kills the process while it works.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="lead-label">A short run is not a failed run.</span> If two frames in a row fall
        below 50% convergence the images have
        <a class="term" href={withBase('/learn/glossary#decorrelation')}>decorrelated</a>
        and Semper stops rather than grind on. The frames it solved before that point are real data —
        they are saved as a shorter analysis, and its Home row records why it stopped. A 50-frame test
        that stopped at frame 40 still gives you frames 1–39.
      </div>
      <p>
        <span class="lead-label">If it fails outright:</span> see
        <a href={withBase('/support/troubleshooting#during-a-run')}>Troubleshooting · during a run</a>
        for the exact messages and what each means.
      </p>

      <h3>Parameters (single mode)</h3>
      <p class="plain">
        The two you actually tune: <strong>subset</strong> (how big a patch each point matches) and
        <strong>strain window</strong> (how much the strain is smoothed). The defaults are sensible —
        the app even recommends a subset from your image.
      </p>
      <Figure
        src="/manual/subset-step.svg"
        alt="Diagram of subset window size versus step spacing on a grid"
        caption="Subset is the match window. Step is how far apart grid points are."
        wide
        width={760}
        height={320}
      />
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Parameter</th>
            <th>Range</th>
            <th>Default / reset</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Subset size</td>
            <td>15–121, odd</td>
            <td>Recommended from your image</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Step size</td>
            <td>1–30</td>
            <td>5</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Strain window</td>
            <td>5–101, odd</td>
            <td>15</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Kernel</td>
            <td>4×4 Bicubic / 6×6 Keys</td>
            <td>4×4 Bicubic</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <Figure
        src="/manual/vsg.svg"
        alt="Virtual strain gauge related to strain window and step"
        caption="Quote VSG with strain results: VSG = (strain window − 1) × step + 1, in pixels."
        wide
        width={760}
        height={270}
      />
      <p>
        Full table with when-to-raise / when-to-lower: <a href={withBase('/learn/parameters')}
          >Parameters</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h3>Region of interest</h3>
      <p class="plain">
        Draw a box over the part of the image you want measured; erase holes over anything that would
        spoil the match, like grips or markers.
      </p>
      <Figure
        src="/manual/screenshots/roi-editor.webp"
        alt="ROI editor with crop rectangle on a speckle image"
        caption="Crop sets the area to correlate. Erase punches out grips or markers that would decorrelate."
        phone
        width={460}
        height={1022}
      />
      <p class="detail">
        <strong>Draw</strong> — pick Rect or Square and drag on the image; drag inside to move, corners
        to resize, with size and position shown live. <strong>Manual</strong> — type X, Y, W and H
        exactly and Apply. <strong>Crop</strong> sets the area to correlate; <strong>Erase</strong>
        punches holes in it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <span class="lead-label">If it fails:</span> an
        <a class="term" href={withBase('/learn/glossary#roi')}>ROI</a> smaller than the subset will not
        run — enlarge the ROI or shrink the subset.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ManualPager
      prev={{ href: '/manual/getting-started', label: 'Getting started' }}
      next={{ href: '/manual/sweeps-results', label: 'Sweeps & results' }}
    />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
